An HTML page's entry root is `@entry.moth`. Entry eligibility follows the file's presence, not the amount of authored runtime work. Direct const root inserts on the entry supply document metadata.

This page describes entry metadata and its relationship to project configuration.

### The `@project` namespace

`@project` is an explicitly bound immutable namespace. The named `project` configuration record supplies its predefined fields. The grouped-record configuration remains the accepted model.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @project version

    footer #= [: v[version]]
]

Normal project modules and eligible project-owned support packages may bind it. It is never implicitly injected or directly re-exported. Project-package exposure restrictions and field provenance still apply. Config helper and input bindings do not become project metadata merely because they exist.

See @#project-config (Project configuration) for the field contract and @#build-inputs (Build configuration values) for independent input resolution.

### HTML builder slots

Use direct top-level const inserts in `@entry.moth` to supply document metadata:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Moth]
    #[$in description:A language for creating reliable software]
    #[$in lang:en]
    #[$in favicon:[@assets/favicon.svg]]
    #[$in stylesheets:[@styles/site.css]]
    #[$in head:<meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff">]
    #[: <main>Hello Moth</main>]
]

The HTML builder registers `title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon`, `body_style`, `stylesheets` and `head`. Ordinary top-level const and runtime fragments render into the body. A nested or aliased `$in` remains a template composition helper, not a builder message. Helper files, API facades and imported modules do not contribute to the selected entry.

An entry with only these direct const contributions still produces a complete document. Runtime top-level templates contribute only to the default body lane. Runtime named builder slots are not supported.

The compiler accepts the active builder's read-only destination schema. It validates that a direct root insert targets a registered name and folds the contribution to a structural value, but it does not know HTML slot meaning. The builder validates slot-specific content and assembles the document. Both the JavaScript and HTML-Wasm paths consume the same builder slot plan, so metadata fallbacks, stylesheet order and body output remain equivalent.

Repeated `title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon` and `body_style` contributions concatenate in source order. Template body whitespace remains part of each value, including a space immediately after `:`. A supplied empty value counts as present. An absent title uses the route, then project name, with the configured title prefix and postfix. An absent description emits no description element. Absent language, favicon and body style use the HTML builder defaults.

The builder escapes title text and attribute values. It inserts `head` as raw HTML after its dedicated head content. It removes only repeated complete head contributions that render identically in the page's URL context. It does not rewrite duplicates inside one contribution.

Each `stylesheets` contribution must contain exactly one tracked CSS resource and optional formatting whitespace. Plain paths, external URLs, site-root values, non-CSS resources and combined resources are invalid. The builder deduplicates by stable resource origin, keeps the first occurrence and renders the URL relative to each output page. Use `head` for an external stylesheet.

Resource and site-root pieces retain their identity through folding and output planning. Named contributions keep their tracked resources live, including on metadata-only pages. The builder does not scan rendered HTML for paths. The former `page_title`, `page_description`, `page_lang`, `page_favicon`, `page_body_style` and `page_head` names are ordinary constants with no implicit document effect.

### Entry selection

`@entry.moth` is the only project entry source. A directory that contains the file is entry-eligible whether the file is empty, static-only, metadata-only or full of runtime work.

`@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` are API-only facades. They never become entries, own no start function and emit no route. An entry-only module publishes no importable API.

Importing a module or binding a package never activates its entry. Only a build or development command activates selected compiled entries. Separately compiled dependency entries stay dormant.

HTML `build` and `dev` require at least one `@entry.moth` anywhere below the selected project directory. No homepage is required. `check` accepts a project with no entries.

